6. CRITICAL: FIX LOCAL MCP NETWORK CONNECTION FAILURES

THIS IS THE MOST IMPORTANT NETWORKING REQUIREMENT.

Previous versions of this project encountered:

"Unable to connect to local network addresses."

This issue must be investigated rather than hidden.

Do not automatically reject private network addresses.

Do not block all local IP addresses.

Do not display a generic connection error when a specific diagnostic is available.

The application must correctly distinguish:

A. Android device localhost.
B. Another device on the same Wi-Fi network.
C. A remote public MCP server.
D. A remote backend or gateway.
E. A desktop computer hosting an MCP service.

Understand these examples:

- 127.0.0.1 means the Android device itself.
- A private LAN address identifies another device on the local network.
- A public hostname may identify a remote server.
- An MCP server running on a desktop is not automatically reachable through Android localhost.

Support user-configured endpoints such as:

- http://127.0.0.1:PORT
- http://192.168.x.x:PORT
- http://10.x.x.x:PORT
- http://172.16.x.x:PORT through 172.31.x.x:PORT
- https://example-hostname

PORT is a user-provided port, not a fixed value.

Validate URLs carefully.

Never automatically block these private IP ranges merely because they are private.

However, private addresses must not bypass authorization, TLS validation, or network security.

